" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "7 – 11 August 1984"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/604"} "Lake Casitas, Los Padres National Forest, California"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/255688"} "19 from 19 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Unlike the 1980 American-led boycott, which had had a limited impact on the canoeing events, the Soviet boycott of the 1984 Summer Olympics severely depleted the field in the sport. This was undeniable in the men’s K-1 1000, for the defending Olympic and four-time World Champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/9824"} "Rüdiger Helm"] " of East Germany was absent, as was the most recent runner-up, " [:a {:href "/athletes/10292"} "Artūras Vieta"] " of the Soviet Union. This left three major contenders at the Games: " [:a {:href "/athletes/10442"} "Alan Thompson"] " of New Zealand, the 1982 World runner-up and 1983 bronze medalist, Norway’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/10412"} "Einar Rasmussen"] ", the 1978 World runner-up and 1981 and 1982 bronze medalist, and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10581"} "Vasile Dîba"] ", a four-time Olympic and seven-time World medalist across various disciplines, who was representing 1981 World runner-up Romania."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Of these three men, only Thompson won his heat, with Dîba and Rasmussen coming in third in heats won by France’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/11060"} "Philippe Boccara"] " and Yugoslavia’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/11201"} "Milan Janić"] ", the 1978 World runner-up, respectively. Boccara and Thompson captured their heats in the semi-finals, with the latter helping to eliminate Rasmussen in the process, while Janić and Dîba were second and third behind Great Britain’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/9749"} "Stephen Jackson"] ". In the medal, Thompson won the gold medal without too much difficulty, while Janić had to stave off a challenge from American " [:a {:href "/athletes/11057"} "Greg Barton"] " to win silver with only half of a second to spare. Barton came in for a surprise third-place finish, while Boccara, Dîba, and Jackson were sixth, seventh, and eighth respectively. Thompson would go on to become a double Olympic champion at the Games by helping his nation win the K-4 1000 later in the day."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "Rep"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "130"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/10442"} "Alan Thompson"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NZL"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NZL.png"}] "NZL"]] [:td {} "3:53.41 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "3:58.90 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "3:45.73 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "170"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/11201"} "Milan Janić"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/YUG"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/YUG.png"}] "YUG"]] [:td {} "3:51.72 (1 h3)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "3:58.43 (2 h3)"] [:td {} "3:46.88 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {:class "bib"} "157"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/11057"} "Greg Barton"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "3:52.08 (2 h3)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "3:54.18 (2 h1)"] [:td {} "3:47.38 (3)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"] [:td {:class "bib"}